/* book Worked example 2: Q: Three aluminium bars meet at a pin D and hang it from a rigid frame. The middle bar is vertical and 2 m long; the outer bars are at 45° to it on either side, so each is 2√2 = 2.828 m long. All bars have A = 300 mm² and E = 70 GPa. A load P = 50 kN hangs from D. Find the force in each bar and the deflection of D.
   1. Count: m = 3, j = 4 (D and three frame pins), r = 6 (two at each frame pin). D = 3 + 6 − 8 = 1, so one bar is redundant. Choose the middle bar, force X.
   2. Cut truss: the two outer bars carry P alone. Vertical equilibrium at D: 2F cos 45° = P − X, so F = (P − X)/√2 in each outer bar.
   3. U = X² × 2/(2AE) + 2 × F² × 2.828/(2AE). Differentiate with ∂F/∂X = −1/√2: ∂U/∂X = [2X − 2 × 2.828 × F/√2]/AE = [2X − 4F]/AE.
   4. Least work, ∂U/∂X = 0: X = 2F = √2(P − X), so X = √2P/(1 + √2) = 0.5858 × 50 = 29.29 kN (tension).
   5. Outer bars: F = X/2 = 14.64 kN each. Check at D: 29.29 + 2 × 14.64 × 0.7071 = 50.0 kN.
   6. Stresses: 29 290/300 = 97.6 MPa in the middle bar, 48.8 MPa in each outer bar.
   7. AE = 300 × 10⁻⁶ × 70 × 10⁹ = 2.1 × 10⁷ N. Deflection of D = stretch of the middle bar = 29 290 × 2/(2.1 × 10⁷) = 2.79 × 10⁻³ m = 2.79 mm.
   8. Compatibility check: each outer bar stretches 14 645 × 2.828/(2.1 × 10⁷) = 1.97 mm = 2.79 × cos 45°, the component of D’s movement along it.
   Answer: Middle bar 29.29 kN, each outer bar 14.64 kN, all in tension; D moves 2.79 mm down. Without the middle bar the outer bars would carry 35.36 kN each and D would drop 6.73 mm. */
